# Whidra - Web-based Reverse Engineering Tool

Inspect web code, review imported captures, and connect evidence in a browser-based workspace. Processing and project storage stay in the browser that opens Whidra. The server serves static assets; imported code is never executed.

## Run

```sh
docker compose up --build -d
```

Open http://localhost:8080. For LAN/HTTPS deployment see [Deployment](docs/DEPLOYMENT.md). The Pi does not need a companion, QEMU, KVM, or a guest image. Browser storage belongs to each origin; export before changing addresses.

For development with Node.js 24:

```sh
npm ci
npm run dev
```

## Investigation tools

Import files/folders or HAR captures. Inspect original source, hex, JavaScript AST, references, Wasm instructions/WAT, function aliases, findings, graphs, and source maps. Beautify and static HTML rendering preserve original evidence. Decode, decompress, and transform bounded samples into derived evidence with lineage; annotate and export a `.whidra.json` project or Markdown report.

Network and Saved observations review imported records. Runtime sessions and breakpoints from legacy projects are historical data; Whidra has no live debugging, replay, remote capture, or sample execution.

## Verification

```sh
npm run typecheck
npm test
npm run build
npx playwright install chromium
npm run test:e2e
```

Use `WRET_BROWSER=msedge` for Edge. Technical WRET storage identifiers remain for compatibility. Browser parsing uses bounded cancellable workers; large files can still require significant client memory.
